Z||000014||jelly2m8||12-20-2001||08:50 PM||||OH GEEZ, looks like another engine operation lesson is in order.<P>1st off, if there is a 4.3L in the Fiero, they would have used a SBC adapter plate.<P>*INTERNAL COMBUSTION ENGINE BASICS 101*   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P><BR> 45* 60* 90* DOES NOT mean neither the transmission bolt pattern, OR the VEE angle of the cylinders.<P> What it does represent is the Degrees of crankshaft rotation for each piston to reach TDC once. Do not confuse this with power stroke.<P> In a 60* V6 the crankshaft makes a 60* turn for each  piston to come up to top dead center (TDC).<P> The crankshaft will turn 360* for all 6 pistons to come to TDC once.<P> In a 90* engine it requires 540* of crankshaft rotation for all 6 pistons to reach TDC once. Thats a turn and a half.<P> The 45* engine (V8) will require 1 turn of the crankshaft for each piston to reach TDC once.<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://users.eastlink.ca/~acook1/pegged%2001.jpg">  <p>[This message has been edited by jelly2m8 (edited 12-20-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000015||Fierobsessed||12-20-2001||10:04 PM||||the Iron duke isnt a very strong its inly designed to hold 92 horses and thats about it. They made a bazillion of them, and there wasnt any real reason to make them perform any better than they do. If you really want to make the duke push some real power, well... you can get about 150 out of it before you really start cutting into the reliability. In reality, The Iron duke is a chevy 302 cut in half, it doesnt share any parts though, just the layout. It actually is very easy to make the motor push some good power, some porting work, a cam, a different intake system and a header will gain you alot of power. The Iron dukes weakest link is definatly the crank. The crank is very lightweight, and has only 1 counterweight per cylender. The forces exerted on the crank are awkward at best and it will break if the RPMs are high enough. The next weakest link is probably the rods. There one of the reason you dont have a 1989 fiero. They were also made in such mas quantities that quality was sacraficed, however, early small block chevys had the same size crank pins and hi perf rods are easy to get, just they have to be modified to fit the iron duke. If you were to fix these two weak links I suppose that would bring up the engines capabilits a long way. But I do not know what the limits are on the stock block.<P>------------------<BR>Matt D<BR>1984 Indy Fiero (auto)<BR>1985 2m4 V6 DIS 3.1 Auto||||reg||14
Z||000016||ITB 2M4||12-21-2001||01:52 AM||||I've had all the internals balanced at a local shop, and had the rods and pistons blueprinted.  Also had the rods treated, and I'm using ARP 283ci Chevy rod bolts on the caps.  I'm turning 5400rpm on a regular basis.  Another important thing is oil if you're gonna turn high rpm's.  I've got an Accusump 3qt tank, a trap door on the oil pan, an oil cooler, and a remote filter, for a grand total of 10qts of oil.  Oil pressure is about 60psi at 5200rpm.  If you're gonna rebuild a motor, make sure you don't use a crank that's been turned more than .010 under (Usually the amount taken off the first time a crank gets turned).  Anymore than that is asking for trouble.<P>------------------<BR>SCCA ITB '84 Fiero 2M4<BR>AOL IM- DGPFiero||||reg||1
Z||000017||artherd||12-21-2001||06:38 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by riceboyfiero:<BR><B>i know they dont make the super duty block anymore...but i have all the confidence in the world that the block can hold 200 ponys</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Well then, you're insane.<P>I had a duke.  threw TWO rods (through the block), crank exploded, and main caps are all gone.  <P>Worst damned motor I've ever had expierence with. <P>You can maybe build one to 120bhp, then baby it.  But a junkyard/used 2.8 will go much harder and last much longer.<P>Best!<BR>Ben.<P>------------------<BR>      <IMG SRC="http://www.cartsys.com/fiero/sig1.jpeg">    <BR>Ben Cannon<BR>88 Formula, T-top, Metalic Red           &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;"Every Man Dies, not every man really Lives"<BR>88 Formula, Northstar, Silver                    &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;-Mel Gibson, "Braveheart"||||reg||1
Z||000018||Mach10||12-21-2001||12:35 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by jelly2m8:<BR><B>OH GEEZ, looks like another engine operation lesson is in order.<P>1st off, if there is a 4.3L in the Fiero, they would have used a SBC adapter plate.<P>*INTERNAL COMBUSTION ENGINE BASICS 101*    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P><BR> 45* 60* 90* DOES NOT mean neither the transmission bolt pattern, OR the VEE angle of the cylinders.<P> What it does represent is the Degrees of crankshaft rotation for each piston to reach TDC once. Do not confuse this with power stroke.<P> In a 60* V6 the crankshaft makes a 60* turn for each  piston to come up to top dead center (TDC).<P> The crankshaft will turn 360* for all 6 pistons to come to TDC once.<P> In a 90* engine it requires 540* of crankshaft rotation for all 6 pistons to reach TDC once. Thats a turn and a half.<P> The 45* engine (V8) will require 1 turn of the crankshaft for each piston to reach TDC once.<P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><BR>I was refering to the block style. If I'm not mistaken, if the 4.3's already in there, then the MOUNTING CHANGES HAVE ALREADY BEEN MADE. I am under the impression that the 3800 and the 4.3L block are very similar, in terms of engine mount locations... By your logic, the GM 60* block will happily bolt in place of the 90*. Er... No. Again, I was refering to the BLOCK STYLE. Wasn't talking about the internals. Get your foot off my throat, or you're gonna LOSE IT &gt;:\<P><BR>||||reg||12
